        550.3589477539062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Reachability Constrained Reinforcement Learning. RCRL [27] is a primal-dual approach where the",
      "bbox": [
        107.69100189208984,
        558.82177734375,
        504.0018615722656,
        567.847900390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "constraint is on the reachability value function and the lagrange multiplier is represented by a neural",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        569.850341796875,
        503.99761962890625,
        578.7568969726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "network parameterized by state.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        580.7593994140625,
        235.16265869140625,
        589.6659545898438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Control Barrier Function. This CBF-based approach is inspired by the various energy-based certifi-",
      "bbox": [
        107.6709976196289,
        597.02880859375,
        505.6541748046875,
        606.054931640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "cation approaches [56, 17, 18, 11, 57, 58]. This is implemented as a primal-dual approach where the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        608.057373046875,
        504.00262451171875,
        616.9639282226562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "control barrier-based constraint ˙h(s) + ν · h(s) ≤0 is to ensure stabilization toward the safe set.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        617.7049560546875,
        492.9715881347656,
        629.39990234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Feasible Actor Critic. FAC [9] is another primal-dual approach similar to RCRL (i.e. it uses the",
      "bbox": [
        107.39199829101562,
        636.7617797851562,
        503.99505615234375,
        645.7879028320312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "NN representation of the lagrange multiplier parameterized by state) except that the constraint in",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        647.7903442382812,
        503.9974365234375,
        656.6968994140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "FAC is based on the cumulative discount sum of costs in lieu of the reachability value function. It is",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        658.580810546875,
        503.99609375,
        667.60693359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "important to note that FAC is originally meant for the CMDP framework (with some positive cost",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        669.4898071289062,
        503.9974060058594,
        678.5159301757812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "threshold), but we adapt it to hard constraints by making the cost threshold χ = 0. We do this to make",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        680.3589477539062,
        504.00128173828125,
        689.4249267578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "a better comparison between an algorithm that relies on using the lagrange multiplier represented as a",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        691.4273681640625,
        504.00152587890625,
        700.3339233398438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "NN to learn feasibility with our approach of using our proposed REF function to learn the feasibility",
      "bbox": [
